<section class="reference-section" id="scripture-library-classification"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Scripture story library · classification notes</h2><a href="/stories.html#collections">Return to library</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>The library distinguishes a story’s exact source from the wider Vedic corpus with which that source is associated. An association does not turn an Upanishadic passage into a Samhita passage.</p><ol><li><strong>Rigveda Samhita.</strong><small>Indra and Vritra follows Rigveda 1.32, a Samhita hymn. Later Puranic Vritra or Vritrasura accounts remain separate.</small></li><li><strong>Samaveda-associated literature.</strong><small>The supplied Kena Upanishad introduction places the text within the Samavedic Talavakara Brahmana. The supplied Chandogya Upanishad preface likewise identifies its Samavedic Talavakara Brahmana setting. The story cards therefore name Kena Upanishad and Chandogya Upanishad as their exact sources.</small></li><li><strong>Shukla Yajurveda-associated literature.</strong><small>The supplied Brihadaranyaka Upanishad preface places the text in the Vajasaneyi Brahmana of the Kanva branch. The Gargi reader remains sourced to Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 3.6 and 3.8.</small></li><li><strong>Krishna Yajurveda-associated literature.</strong><small>The supplied Katha Upanishad preface identifies the text with the Katha branch of the Krishna Yajurveda. The Nachiketa reader remains sourced to the Katha Upanishad, not to the Yajurveda Samhita.</small></li><li><strong>Devi Mahatmya.</strong><small>The three current Devi Mahatmya readers are cross-listed under their embedded text and under its parent Markandeya Purana. They retain one canonical story page each.</small></li><li><strong>Related Puranic passages.</strong><small>Halahala and Markandeya–Shiva appear in more than one relevant collection only where the source notes already document a distinct account or related passage. The cards continue to identify the canonical reader’s textual boundary.</small></li></ol><p>Supported but presently empty classifications—Atharvaveda-associated literature, Linga Purana and Mahabharata—are retained in the data registry and withheld from the public collection chooser until an exact verified story source exists.</p></div></section>
